  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nThe cloud coverage today has been very stagnant and not \nclearing out at all, instead it has been backbuilding into the \nCWA more progressively toward the northwest and decreased some \nin cloud heights. These clouds will eventually shift out toward \nthe east tonight as a moisture starved cold frontal passage \ntakes place. This feature will pass through tonight and into \nMonday morning and exit toward mid to late morning. As surface \nflow drops off and the clouds clear out for the most part, \nvalley fog will become a possibility for the overnight and into \nMonday morning, especially across the deeper mountain valleys. \n\nStrong high pressure building in post frontal passage will\nmaintain dry conditons along with below seasonable to \nseasonable through the rest of the work week. This dry weather \nalong with dead surface flow for the overnights will promote \nthe possibility of frost for Tuesday morning and possibly some \non Wednesday morning across the northeast mountains where lows \nin the morning could drop down into the low to mid 30s. \nTemperatures then moderate slightly for the second half of the \nwork week to diminish the chances for anymore frost potential. \n\nThe next chance for precipitation will be on Sunday where \nmedium range models have a system traveling to our region from \nthe Gulf. Grater chances for rain are now being advertised by \ncentral guidance on this day.",
